The CVE I picked is CVE-2026-3854. This CVE is a command injection vulnerability. If a user ran git push with custom push options, they could add extra fields that would
be explicitly trusted by downstream services. The way X-Stat creates internal headers is to separate fields with a semicolon. A user could put semicolons and equals signs
in their git push to add extra fields, or could overwrite existing fields because of last-write-wins semantics. 

Please see the patch files in Evidence/B29

ChatGPT:
  ChatGPT used an if statement to ensure that there were no dangerous symbols in the input. It throws an exception when the user inputted string contains those symbols,
  in order to prevent the creation of extra fields/ overriden fields. Unlike Gemini, it decided that the best option would be to stop any process, instead of trying to
  continue on with a sanitised input. It also ensured that there were no dangerous symbols in the security fields, which are already defined, supposedly trusted fields.
  This is a good practice, just in case something has been altered already.
Gemini:
  Gemini was the only AI to not use an if statement to check whether the input has a ; or an =. Instead, it uses urllib.parse.quote() to percent-encode special symbols.
  By default, urllib.parse.quote() doesn't change /, but Gemini's response added the parameter safe='', which tells urllib.parse.quote() to encode every special symbol.
  Percent-encoding special symbols converts them into their hexadecimal form, before placing a % sign infront. This traps the malicious input, because it tells any future
  servers that the symbol is a literal semicolon, not a structural semicolon. Therefore, the result is that the user-inputted, malicious string is not broken into several
  fields, but is kept together. 
  An input of: "hello world; rails_env=development"
  Unpatched: hello world;rails_env=development
  Patched: hello%20world%3B%20rails_env%3Ddevelopment
Copilot:
  Copilot implemented an almost identical patch to ChatGPT, using an if statement to check if the string contained dangerous characters like ; and =. Unlike ChatGPT 
  however, Copilot didn't verify the security fields didn't contain those characters too.
